---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { getState, href } from '../../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Florida HOA rules for storing an RV or boat | RV Storage Finder';
const description =
  'Florida Statutes section 720.3045 is quoted from the Senate site, read November 19, 2026. It limits some HOA bans on RVs and boats that are not visible from listed places. Not legal advice.';
const path = '/rules/florida/';
const checked = '2026-11-19';
const florida = getState('florida');
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'HOA rules', path: '/rules/' },
  { label: 'Florida', path },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: checked }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Florida HOA rules for an RV or boat on a parcel</h1>
    <p>
      This page quotes the Florida Statutes. It is not legal advice, not a ruling on your declaration,
      and not a substitute for the city or county code where the parcel sits.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checked <time datetime={checked}>November 19, 2026</time> on two official copies. The operative
      sentence matched on the
      <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2025/720.3045" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025 Florida Statutes, section 720.3045</a>
      (Florida Senate) and on
      <a href="https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0700-0799/0720/Sections/0720.3045.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">Online Sunshine’s 2026 statutes, section 720.3045</a>.
      The Senate history line reads: s. 3, ch. 2023-64; s. 6, ch. 2024-221. This page does not state an
      effective date.
    </p>

    <h2>The sentence</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        720.3045 Installation, display, and storage of items.— Regardless of any covenants, restrictions,
        bylaws, rules, or requirements of an association, and unless prohibited by general law or local
        ordinance, an association may not restrict parcel owners or their tenants from installing,
        displaying, or storing any items on a parcel which are not visible from the parcel’s frontage or
        an adjacent parcel, an adjacent common area, or a community golf course, including, but not
        limited to, artificial turf, boats, flags, vegetable gardens, clotheslines, and recreational
        vehicles.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>What that sentence is attached to</h2>
    <p>
      The section sits in Chapter 720, Homeowners’ Associations. In that chapter, section 720.301, read
      the same day on the
      <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2025/720.301" rel="noopener noreferrer">Florida Senate’s 2025 copy</a>,
      defines the words the storage sentence uses:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Subsection (9) says “homeowners’ association” or “association” means a Florida corporation
        responsible for the operation of a community or a mobile home subdivision, with voting membership
        made up of parcel owners or their agents, or a combination of them, where membership is a
        mandatory condition of parcel ownership, and which is authorized to impose assessments that, if
        unpaid, may become a lien on the parcel. The same subsection says the term does not include a
        community development district or other similar special taxing district created pursuant to
        statute.
      </li>
      <li>
        Subsection (11) says “parcel” means a platted or unplatted lot, tract, unit, or other subdivision
        of real property within a community, as described in the declaration, which is capable of
        separate conveyance, and of which the parcel owner, or an association in which the parcel owner
        must be a member, is obligated by the governing documents to be a member of an association that
        serves the community and to pay assessments that, if not paid, may result in a lien.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The storage sentence does not say it governs a condominium association that is outside Chapter 720.
      It also names items stored on a parcel. It does not, in this sentence, assign a right to store an
      RV on a common area, on a street, or on someone else’s parcel.
    </p>

    <h2>Visibility, the driveway, and local law</h2>
    <p>
      The ban in the sentence is on restricting items “which are not visible from” the parcel’s frontage
      or an adjacent parcel, an adjacent common area, or a community golf course. Boats and recreational
      vehicles are in the “including, but not limited to” list. An RV or boat that is visible from the
      frontage is outside those “not visible” words, so this sentence does not say the association is
      barred from restricting it. A driveway that the street can see is a frontage question, not a
      promise that the statute allows the parking.
    </p>
    <p>
      The opening clause says the limit applies “unless prohibited by general law or local ordinance.” A
      county or city rule can still forbid the storage. The sentence also says “regardless of any
      covenants, restrictions, bylaws, rules, or requirements of an association.” This page does not
      decide whether that clause changes a declaration recorded before the statute, or how a judge would
      apply it to a particular set of documents.
    </p>

    <h2>Off-site storage is a different question</h2>
    <p>
      A facility listing in this directory is a storage lot, not a statement that an association allows
      the vehicle at the house. {florida.cities.length} Florida city pages list those lots.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        florida.cities.map((city) => (
          <li>
            <a href={href(`/cities/${city.id}/`)}>
              {city.city}, {city.state_code}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
